{"h":3395,"lines":[{"y":0.046511627815394085,"h":0.0088476182715210294,"t":"PE 1718867188","c":1,"x":0.7716049391467531,"w":0.069958845774332645},{"c":1,"t":"Audioscripts","w":0.11321458180745442,"h":0.014714088260214808,"x":0.11520340857351841,"y":0.053689467482791797},{"w":0.69341562906901044,"y":0.097383720823917774,"h":0.011627907185728592,"c":1,"t":"heated until the sap boils. As it does this, the water evaporates, and the syrup begins to form.","x":0.11728395490477908},{"w":0.68930043538411456,"t":"The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam, and the sap becomes thicker and","h":0.011778563015312105,"c":1,"x":0.11728393858366284,"y":0.11189634877433463},{"x":0.1172839467370189,"y":0.12632508820670107,"w":0.69135803222656245,"t":"denser, and, at just the right moment, when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup,","c":1,"h":0.011778563015312216},{"c":1,"t":"the worker removes it from the heat. After this process, something called 'sugar sand' has to","y":0.13953488359984101,"x":0.11728395585906924,"h":0.013081395583944722,"w":0.68724278767903646},{"x":0.11728394140775199,"w":0.67078191121419273,"h":0.010306242638398189,"c":1,"t":"be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance","y":0.15547703172215621},{"x":0.11728395491613269,"t":"and a slightly gritty taste. Once this has been done, the syrup is ready to be packaged so that","h":0.013081395583944611,"c":1,"y":0.16860465104304945,"w":0.69341562906901033},{"x":0.11728395681305367,"y":0.18313953477712974,"c":1,"h":0.011627907185728703,"t":"it can be used for a whole variety of products. It takes 40 litres of sap to produce one litre of","w":0.68106994628906248},{"y":0.19620748326687987,"w":0.44878362019856777,"h":0.015378557751119848,"t":"maple syrup so you can get an idea of how much is needed!","x":0.11922530213511218,"c":1},{"t":"So that's the basic process. In places like Quebec where ...","y":0.21937573603959071,"w":0.44032923380533862,"h":